# How Do I Grow Feijoa in My Garden or Backyard?

## Short Answer

Feijoa (Acca sellowiana), also known as pineapple guava, is a hardy evergreen shrub or small tree that produces aromatic, egg-shaped fruit with a sweet-tart flavor reminiscent of pineapple, strawberry, and guava.
It is one of the most cold-tolerant subtropical fruit plants, surviving temperatures down to about -10°C (14°F) once established.
Growing feijoa is straightforward in USDA zones 8-11, and it integrates beautifully into [[backyard-orchard-design]], edible landscaping, and [[fruit-tree-guild-roles]] in permaculture systems.

## Climate and Hardiness

Feijoa is native to southern Brazil, Uruguay, Paraguay, and northern Argentina, where it grows in subtropical highland conditions.
Unlike true tropical guavas such as [[psidium-guajava]], feijoa thrives in temperate climates with cool winters and warm summers.
It requires at least 50-100 chill hours for reliable flowering, making it suitable for regions that experience mild winter chilling.
The plant tolerates maritime winds and coastal salt spray, making it an excellent candidate for [[windbreak-and-shelterbelt-design]] in coastal permaculture systems.
In colder zones, feijoa can be grown in large containers and moved indoors or into a greenhouse during hard freezes.
For marginal climates, [[frost-protection-strategies]] such as frost cloth and [[microclimate-design-for-garden-and-farm]] placement near south-facing walls can extend the viable growing range.

## Variety Selection

Several cultivated varieties offer improved fruit size, flavor, and yield compared to seed-grown plants.
Most feijoas are partially self-sterile, meaning planting two or more different varieties dramatically improves fruit set through cross-pollination.
Popular varieties include Mammoth, Triumph, Apollo, and Gemini, each with slightly different fruit characteristics and harvest times.
Choose varieties that are known to flower simultaneously to ensure successful cross-pollination between plants.
The species [[feijoa-sellowiana]] produces reliable ornamental value with edible fruit, though named cultivars generally produce larger, better-tasting fruit.
Some nurseries sell grafted plants that begin fruiting within 1-2 years, compared to 3-5 years for seedlings.

## Site Selection and Soil Preparation

Feijoas prefer full sun to partial shade, with at least 6-8 hours of direct sunlight producing the best fruit yields.
They adapt to a wide range of soil types but prefer well-drained, slightly acidic to neutral soil with a pH between 5.5 and 7.0.
Understanding [[soil-ph-and-nutrient-availability]] helps ensure optimal nutrient uptake, particularly for iron and manganese in alkaline soils.
Heavy clay soils should be improved with organic matter and [[mulching-guide]] amendments to enhance drainage and root development.
Incorporating [[sheet-mulching]] techniques during bed preparation creates a rich, friable planting medium without tilling.
Space plants 3-5 meters apart for hedge-style plantings or 5-8 meters apart for freestanding specimens, depending on pruning goals.
Feijoa works well as a [[hedgerow-planting]] element, providing privacy, wind shelter, and food simultaneously.

## Planting and Establishment

Plant feijoas in spring after the danger of frost has passed, or in autumn in mild-winter regions where roots can establish before summer heat.
Dig a hole twice as wide as the root ball but no deeper than the root crown, keeping the graft union above soil level if applicable.
Water thoroughly at planting and apply a 5-10 cm layer of organic mulch, keeping it a few centimeters away from the trunk to prevent rot.
Young plants benefit from [[drip-irrigation]] systems that deliver consistent moisture to the root zone without wetting the foliage.
Regular watering during the first growing season is critical, as young feijoas are shallow-rooted and sensitive to drought stress.
Once established after 1-2 years, feijoas become remarkably [[drought-tolerant-planting-design]] subjects that need minimal supplemental water.

## Pruning and Training

Feijoas respond well to both formal and informal pruning, making them suitable for [[espalier-fruit-tree-training-and-design]] along walls and fences.
Light annual pruning in late winter or early spring maintains shape, removes dead wood, and encourages new fruiting wood.
Unlike many fruit trees, feijoas fruit on the current season's growth, so moderate pruning does not sacrifice the next harvest.
Overgrown specimens can be renovation-pruned by removing one-third of the oldest branches per year over three years.
For hedging, shear after fruiting to maintain density and form, which aligns with the guidance in [[fruit-tree-pruning-and-training-systems]].
Maintain an open center to improve air circulation and light penetration, reducing disease pressure in humid climates.

## Fertilizing and Nutrient Management

Apply a balanced organic fertilizer in early spring as new growth begins, following the schedule in an [[organic-fruit-tree-care-calendar]].
Avoid high-nitrogen fertilizers that promote excessive vegetative growth at the expense of flowering and fruit production.
Feijoas are light feeders compared to citrus and stone fruits, so moderate applications of compost or well-rotted manure are usually sufficient.
Micronutrient deficiencies, particularly of zinc and boron, can reduce fruit set and quality in alkaline soils.
Adjusting pH with [[soil-ph-management]] amendments improves the availability of these trace elements naturally.
[[chop-and-drop-mulching]] with comfrey, yarrow, or other accumulator plants around the drip line feeds the soil food web and recycles minerals.

## Pest and Disease Management

Feijoas are relatively pest-resistant compared to most fruit trees, which is one reason they feature prominently in low-spray and organic orchards.
Common issues include scale insects, thrips, and fruit flies in warmer regions where these pests are endemic.
Encouraging [[beneficial-insects-pollinators]] through nearby flowering companion plants helps keep pest populations in check naturally.
The thick fruit skin provides some protection against fruit fly oviposition, making feijoa less susceptible than peaches or figs.
Fungal diseases such as leaf spot can occur in humid climates but rarely cause serious damage to healthy plants.
Good air circulation from proper spacing and pruning, as described in [[fruit-tree-care-and-orchard-management]], minimizes foliar disease issues.

## Harvesting and Using the Fruit

Feijoa fruit typically ripens in autumn, falling from the tree when fully mature, which makes harvesting as simple as gathering fallen fruit.
Unlike many fruits, feijoas do not ripen well on the tree and are best harvested by shaking the tree or collecting daily drops.
Ripe fruit yields slightly to gentle pressure and has a fragrant, tropical aroma from the jelly-like interior.
The entire fruit is edible including the skin, though many people prefer to scoop out the pulp and discard the sometimes astringent peel.
Feijoas can be eaten fresh, added to fruit salads, blended into smoothies, or cooked into jams, chutneys, and desserts.
For longer storage, the pulp freezes well and can be preserved using techniques from [[canning-and-preserving]] guides.
A single mature plant can produce 20-50 kg of fruit per year, making feijoa a highly productive addition to any food garden.

## What About Growing Feijoa in Containers?

Feijoas adapt well to container growing, making them suitable for patios, balconies, and regions colder than their hardiness zone.
Choose a large container of at least 40-50 liters with good drainage holes and use a high-quality potting mix blended with compost.
Container-grown feijoas require more frequent watering and feeding than in-ground plants, as the limited root volume dries out faster.
Dwarf or compact varieties are ideal for containers, though any feijoa can be maintained at a manageable size with regular pruning.
Move containers to a sheltered position or greenhouse during extended freezes, using [[frost-protection-in-permaculture]] techniques for added protection.
Container feijoas typically produce less fruit than in-ground specimens but still reward growers with a respectable harvest within 2-3 years.

## What About Feijoa in Permaculture Systems?

Feijoa's multiple functions make it an excellent permaculture plant that provides food, shelter, wind protection, and ornamental value simultaneously.
Its dense evergreen foliage creates [[microclimate-site-assessment]] benefits by reducing wind speed and providing shade for understory plants.
Feijoa integrates naturally into food forest designs, occupying the shrub or small-tree layer beneath canopy species.
As noted in [[feijoa-and-ugni-cool-climate-myrtaceous-fruits]], it belongs to the Myrtaceae family alongside other useful permaculture plants.
Plant feijoa with [[companion-planting-guild]] species such as comfrey, nasturtiums, and flowering herbs that attract pollinators and suppress weeds.
The fallen fruit provides food for poultry and wildlife, closing nutrient loops in integrated [[fruit-tree-guild-roles]] systems.

## Key Points

- Feijoa (Acca sellowiana) is a cold-hardy subtropical fruit plant suitable for USDA zones 8-11 and beyond with protection.
- Plant at least two different varieties for reliable cross-pollination and significantly better fruit set.
- [[feijoa-sellowiana]] adapts to many soil types but prefers well-drained, slightly acidic conditions.
- Feijoas are drought-tolerant once established and integrate well into [[drought-tolerant-planting-design]] schemes.
- The fruit is nutritious, aromatic, and versatile for fresh eating, cooking, and preserving.
- Pruning is light and flexible, suitable for [[espalier-fruit-tree-training-and-design]] or informal hedging.
- Relatively pest- and disease-resistant, feijoas fit naturally into organic and low-spray orchard systems.
- Container growing extends the range into colder climates with proper winter protection.

